  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 213 AM EDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nCurrent radar depicts a line of storms which crossed over the St. \nMarys River and into southern Ontario CAN this morning. This line \nhas outrun the frontal boundary behind it, which is reaching the \neastern part of Lk Superior early this morning (just after \nmidnight). This trailing weak boundary is reaching down into Lk MI, \nand will continue to veer winds to the west/northwest this morning. \nIt will take until this afternoon for drier air to filter over \nnorthern MI, which will result in lingering elevated instability and \nthus chances for isolated showers and storms. The environment is not \ncapable of stronger storms, with the largest threat being brief \nheavy rain and thunder. NE lower will be where these chances hang on \nuntil midday, with NW Lower and easter upper seeing most chances \nexit by then. \n\nSurface high pressure will sink southward, down the Ohio River \nValley. Surface ridging will allow temperatures to warm into the \nhigh 70s to low 80s under mostly sunny skies (especailly by the late \nafternoon hours). \n\nStarting tonight, southerly winds over the central plains will \nadvect moisture and warmer air northward. This will delineate a warm \nfrontal boundary draped somewhere over IA/MN/WI. With broad quasi-\nzonal flow aloft, lee side lows over the rockies will get a chance \nto form each afternoon (Sunday and Monday). Models are currently \nhaving a hard time developing and progressing these surface \nlows, which points to a scenario where the Gulf moisture tap is\npointed towards MI (up the Ohio River Valley) for a couple of \ndays. The warm frontal boundary/stationary boundary will reside \nsomewhere over MN/IA/WI/MI (draped west to east) for the \nmajority of the weekend and even into next week. \n\nNorth of this, Sunday the beginnings of a cool CAN front will push \ninto the U.P. and provide another chance for showers and storms \nbeginning early Sunday. The cool front boundary and the stagnant \nwarm frontal boundary will meet (likely over WI/MI) Sunday and \ngenerate scattered showers and storms over much of the state. Intial \nlook at afternoon RAP soundings show deep moisture/skinny CAPE \nprofiles, with steep lower level lapse rates. Sfc-3km shear of \naround 25 to 30kts and up to 1000J/kg of SBCAPE will keep the\nchance of severe threats low.. with a likely fate of pulse type\nconvection for most storms during the afternoon. Going into the\nevening and early night time hours, the warm front could surge \nnorthward and reach M-72. This would bring a much more ideal \nenvironment for storm development and strengthening over areas \njust along and to the south of that boundary. It is still too \nearly to know if this boundary will reach the northern part of \nthe state, and history points to most of the convection forming\nearlier over southern WI/MI. This would choke off moisture to \nthe northern part of the L.P., keeping the boundary south. \nNonetheless, confidence does exist for some scattered showers \nand storms Sunday over much of northern MI. Stronger storms \nwill be capable of at least lightning and heavy rain. Training \nof storms producing heavy rain will lead to flooding over low \nlying areas (steering winds likely parallel to boundary).\n\nDepending on what happens Sunday, another round of showers and \nstorms will be possible Monday as the warm front continues to linger \nover MN/WI/IL/MI. Midweek, a couple of weak waves from CAN will \nattempt to push cooler CAN air over Lk Superior and into MI. This \ncould turn winds to the north/northwest and breifly move the \nmoisture to the southern part of the state.. Keeping northern MI dry \nfor the end of the work week. Signals are starting to show a larger \nupper trough seeping over the eastern Great Lakes next weekend, \nwhich could cooler temperatures down to near or just below normal. \nLots of potential for that to change.",
